{
  "title": "Roman to Integer",
  "description": "Convert the Roman numeral `s` to an integer.\n\n| Symbol | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| `I` | 1 |\n| `V` | 5 |\n| `X` | 10 |\n| `L` | 50 |\n| `C` | 100 |\n| `D` | 500 |\n| `M` | 1000 |\n\nSymbols are normally written from largest to smallest and added together. Six subtractive pairs are used instead of repeating a symbol four times: `IV` (4), `IX` (9), `XL` (40), `XC` (90), `CD` (400) and `CM` (900).\n\n### Examples\n\n| `s` | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| `\"III\"` | `3` |\n| `\"LVIII\"` | `58` |\n| `\"MCMXCIV\"` | `1994` |\n\n### Constraints\n\n- `1 <= s.size() <= 15`\n- `s` contains only the characters `I`, `V`, `X`, `L`, `C`, `D` and `M`, and is a valid numeral between 1 and 3999.\n- Inputs are passed as function arguments and the answer is returned from the function. Do not read from standard input or print the result.\n",
  "reference": "int roman_to_int(string s) {\n    auto value = [](char c) {\n        switch (c) {\n            case 'I': return 1;\n            case 'V': return 5;\n            case 'X': return 10;\n            case 'L': return 50;\n            case 'C': return 100;\n            case 'D': return 500;\n            default: return 1000;\n        }\n    };\n    int total = 0;\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < s.size(); ++i) {\n        int current = value(s[i]);\n        if (i + 1 < s.size() && value(s[i + 1]) > current) {\n            total -= current;\n        } else {\n            total += current;\n        }\n    }\n    return total;\n}\n",
  "template": "int roman_to_int(string s) {\n    // Return the integer value of the Roman numeral s.\n    return 0;\n}\n",
